y  be  quite easily detectable with both  workflows but  as genes get less and less abundant  the targeted sequencing workflow is  really advantageous because it can  bump up the efficiency at which at which  you can detect  um intermediately or lowly expressed  genes and so um  if the question is sort of how how well  does it work across cell types the  question  should be probably rephrased in how  similar are those cells in terms of  their  transcriptomic profile because that is  sort of the key determinant  um the other the other key determinant  and i didn't spend  much time on that in in the seminar is  that you need to have a good  understanding of where transcription  stops  because you're amplifying with a primer  the three prime end  of a gene and if your three prime  end is very poorly or not at all  annotated  and you're several kilobases away from  where you think that gene ends  it's it's very difficult to pick it up  by a pcr-based approach  so you need to have a good bulk rna  sequencing data set to start with  um to sort of maximize your chances of  picking up  all the genes that you would like to  pick up  okay thank you i have another question  there are a few cases where targeted  sequencing delivers less reads per cell  than  unbiased sequencing what is the reason  for this  well um the reason for this is  that it's still it's still a pcr-based  approach so one of the reasons is that  is the reason that i just alluded to  which is  you may not have annotated your  transcriptional termination signal  correctly  but this you can largely overcome by  performing  rna sequencing prior to designing your  primers  the other possibility is that  that the algorithm your primary design  algorithm fails  we've seen a few cases of that in the  data and you may have noticed that there  were  very few dots of the roughly  sort of 220 genes that we amplified  where whole transcriptome sequencing was  more successful in picking up the gene  than targeted sequencing  there are few such cases but we haven't  encountered many so that there's  definitely i would say less than  less than five percent of all the genes  we target  um seem to be difficult to to sort of  pick up because of  issues with the primary design  all right thank you i'm going to move on  to another question  how long does the workflow on the  rhapsody take  yeah i guess that question can probably  go to me so i'm going to going to answer  this one  remember we want to be nice to ourselves  so we want to be  um we want to to apply the least bias  to ourselves and not give them a lot of  time  to to react by transcriptomic change  um um upon the upon the the experimental  or the workflow conditions so what i'm  saying here is we keep them  in physiological buffer we just let them  sit on the graph on this um  cartridge and settle them down by  gravity this certainly takes some time  so the typical  incubation time on the cartridges is 15  minutes  and then once the cells are settled in  there they are  the beads are added to it which takes  about another let's say three to five  minutes um which adds up to 20 and then  we still do the scanning which also adds  a few minutes to it  so in roughly i would say between lysis  between loading of the cartridge and  lies as we we are looking at  something like 25 to 30 minutes  and then once once the cells are lysed  obviously the mrna the transcriptome is  captured by the beads and nothing  changes anymore  okay i have time for one more question  and i want to remind our audience that  those questions we were unable to answer  today and those that come in during the  on-demand period will be answered  via the email address you provided at  the time of registration  our last question i am interested in  protein analysis and you spoke about  absec  how many proteins can you analyze  simultaneously  yeah i guess um that question i will  take happily take on it  again um absolute yes absent is uh  interrogating the surface markers of the  cells  that are being expressed on the cells  and other than with  inflow cytometry where we um where the  detection goes via rear light so we are  relying relying on the emission spectrum  of the of the dye and hence we are sort  of limited in terms of  um mixing too many dyes to each other  with with each other because the  emission spectra at some point will  overlap and hamper the analysis  this is not the case when you design uh  when you when you're able to couple  oligonucleotides to your antibodies and  you can design them bioinformatically  before you do so  so long answer short we  support a panels as complex as 100  blacks  and i do know of customers who do more  than that  which at the moment is not officially  supported but  we know it works  well thank you again uh dr kyle hulse dr  zajac and dr burke summer do you have  any final comments for our audience  yeah um oh go ahead  i i just want to re-emphasize that i  think this  is at my point of view um the targeted  sequencing workflow on the video  rhapsody is really  for us has become a game changer because  it really allows  um sort of screens at a different level  you know we've done hundred thousand  cells in one day last week  and um um i really think that  um um this will make a difference and  will  will sort of um change the way we we  conduct these experiments  and i just wanted to mention that it's  been fantastic to work with  all of you so thank you very much and i  also wanted to encourage all the  all the listeners please don't hesitate  to get in touch with any of us  in case you have any questions about  single cell sequencing  of sequencing or the crop seek method  we're more than happy to  to assist yeah and a few final words  from myself  as well before they say thing thanks to  all of you  um working for bd biosciences  um with with such an innovation uh  innovative um surrounding and and  so partnering with with people like like  gentlemen like like  pavel here is is really great fun and um  we as spd we really want to support  the biology and advanced biology by  bringing these innovative tools to  market and as  i'm very happy to see the use cases that  are coming out of this  as we have just it has has just been  demonstrated by gretchen  thanks a lot  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  and once again questions we did not have  time for today and those submitted  during the on-demand period  will be addressed by the speaker via the  contact information that you provided  at the time of registration we would  like to once again thank our speakers  for their time today and for their  important research we would also like to  thank  lab roots and our sponsors illumina and  bd biosciences for underwriting today's  educational webcast  you can visit the webinar on demand lab  roots will alert you via email when it's  available for replay  that's all for now until next time  goodbye
 

